menu "Deepgram Agent Device"

    config WIFI_SSID
        string "Wi-Fi SSID (first-boot seed)"
        default ""
        help
            SSID of the 2.4 GHz network to join. The ESP32-S3 has no 5 GHz radio.

            A SEED, NOT A SETTING. It is used only when nothing has been saved
            yet. Once the device has been provisioned -- through the setup
            portal, or from an earlier boot that used this seed -- the saved
            network wins and editing this appears to do nothing at all.

            Leave it empty to go straight to the setup portal on first boot,
            which is the right choice for any device that leaves the bench.

            To get back here: hold BOOT for 3 seconds, or erase NVS.

    config WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Wi-Fi password (first-boot seed)"
        default ""
        help
            WPA2 passphrase for WIFI_SSID. Leave empty for an open network.

            Same precedence rule as WIFI_SSID: a saved password wins over this.

    config WIFI_MAX_RETRY
        int "Wi-Fi connect attempts before giving up"
        default 8
        range 1 100

    config DEEPGRAM_API_KEY
        string "Deepgram API key"
        default ""
        help
            Sent as "Authorization: Token <key>" on the WebSocket upgrade request.
            Create one at https://console.deepgram.com.

            A SEED, NOT A SETTING -- the same rule as WIFI_SSID. It is used only
            when nothing has been saved yet. Once a key has been entered in the
            setup portal, the saved key wins and editing this appears to do
            nothing at all.

            Leave it empty for any device that leaves the bench: the portal asks
            for the key alongside the network, so a device can be handed to
            someone else without handing over a key or a toolchain.

            To get back here: wipe NVS. Forgetting the network with a BOOT hold
            does NOT erase the key -- the two live in separate namespaces on
            purpose, so re-provisioning Wi-Fi does not cost you the key.

            A wrong key is now visible rather than silent: the device says
            "check api key" and stops retrying, instead of reconnecting forever
            the way a bad network does.


    config DEEPGRAM_FLUX_VOICE
        string "Flux TTS voice"
        default "flux-kit-en"
        help
            The FACTORY DEFAULT voice. It is used on first boot and after an NVS
            erase; once the user changes the voice by asking the agent, the saved
            choice wins on every later boot and this value only comes back via
            the agent's reset_voice function.

            Any name in the catalog in main/voices.c works, not just the thirteen
            offered to the model -- that is where the Irish, Australian, Indian,
            Singaporean and Filipino accents live.

    config DEEPGRAM_FLUX_EOT_THRESHOLD
        string "Flux end-of-turn confidence threshold"
        default ""
        help
            0.0-1.0, as a string (e.g. "0.7"). Higher waits for more confidence
            before ending a turn: fewer interruptions, slower replies.

            Leave empty to let the server choose, which is the right starting
            point -- tune only after hearing the default behave badly.

    config DEEPGRAM_FLUX_EOT_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Flux end-of-turn timeout (ms)"
        default 0
        range 0 10000
        help
            How long silence may run before a turn is force-ended. 0 omits the
            field and uses the server default.

    config DEEPGRAM_LOG_WIRE_JSON
        bool "Log every JSON message sent to Deepgram"
        default n
        help
            Prints the full Settings / UpdateSpeak / FunctionCallResponse bodies.
            Verbose, and Settings contains no secret (the API key rides in an HTTP
            header, not the body), but it is the fastest way to check that what
            you think you are sending is what actually goes on the wire.

    config AGENT_NAME
        string "Agent name"
        default "Grammer"
        help
            What the agent is called. Reaches the model as {{name}} in
            main/prompt/identity.md, not as a function schema -- it is one
            string, not a catalog.

            THE FACTORY DEFAULT, with the same precedence rule as the voice: it
            is used on first boot and after an NVS erase, and once the user has
            asked to be calling it something else the saved name wins on every
            later boot. The agent's reset_name function is what brings this value
            back.

            Kept short on purpose. It is spoken aloud, and a name arrives from
            whatever a stranger said out loud, so main/agent_name.h caps it at
            31 characters -- long enough for a name, too short to be a paragraph
            of smuggled instructions.


    config DEEPGRAM_AGENT_GREETING
        string "Agent greeting"
        default "I'm here... just talk to me like a person."
        help
            Spoken by the agent as soon as the session starts. Because the greeting
            needs no microphone input, hearing audio frames arrive is the quickest
            proof that the whole pipeline is connected.

            IT IS ALSO THE PERSONA'S COLD OPEN, and the only line in this build
            the model does not write. main/prompt/ spends two thousand words on
            not sounding like a product, so a greeting that announces the hardware
            undoes the first impression before the first reply. Keep it in voice:
            short, low-key, no pitch, no feature list.

            Spoken-safe, like everything else the device says -- no markdown, no
            brackets, and no dashes, which are read out. "..." is the pause.

            SHORT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE HERE. There is no barge-in on this board,
            so every second of greeting is a second the listener cannot interrupt.

            Sent only when there is no history to replay, so a session reopened by
            a voice change does not greet again.

    config AUDIO_OUT_VOLUME
        int "Speaker volume (0-100)"
        default 100
        range 0 100
        help
            The FACTORY DEFAULT level, used on first boot and after an NVS erase.
            Once the user asks the agent to change the volume, the saved level
            wins on every later boot. The runtime range is clamped to 20-100 --
            0 is silence rather than quiet, and an agent that has muted itself
            cannot be asked to unmute.

            Beware what "100" means: esp_codec_dev's default volume curve maps
            0-100 onto -50 dB to 0 dB, so 100 is unity gain, NOT the loudest the
            hardware goes. The ES8311's DAC volume register reaches +32 dB. If
            100 is still too quiet, that headroom is what
            AUDIO_OUT_EXTRA_GAIN_DB opens up.

    config AUDIO_OUT_RANGE_DB
        int "Usable volume range (dB below maximum)"
        default 20
        range 6 50
        help
            How far below maximum the volume scale reaches. Volume 0 sits this
            many dB under volume 100, and the scale is linear in dB between them
            -- which is the perceptually even axis, so equal steps sound like
            equal changes.

            This exists because esp_codec_dev's stock curve spans 50 dB, and on
            this board most of that is inaudible. Measured on the speaker:

                effective  -36 dB  -16 dB  -11 dB   -6 dB   -1 dB  +4 dB
                stock vol      20      60      70      80      90    100
                            <-- essentially silent -->  low  medium  max

            Sixty of the hundred steps did nothing. 20 dB of range puts the
            whole 20-100 travel inside the part you can actually hear.

            Widen it if you want a quieter minimum, narrow it if the bottom of
            the range is still too quiet to be useful.

    config AUDIO_OUT_EXTRA_GAIN_DB
        int "Extra speaker gain above unity (dB)"
        default 0
        range 0 24
        help
            Replaces the default volume curve so that AUDIO_OUT_VOLUME=100 maps
            to this many dB instead of 0 dB, reaching into the ES8311's +32 dB
            range.

            This is digital gain applied to the PCM, so it clips: speech has a
            high crest factor and survives a few dB, but push far enough and
            loud passages distort before quiet ones get usefully louder. Raise
            it in ~6 dB steps and stop at the first sign of harshness.

            0 keeps the stock curve.

    config MIC_IN_GAIN
        int "Microphone ADC gain (dB)"
        default 24
        range 0 48
        help
            spec_analyzer_radial uses 24 dB on this board. Raise it if mic peaks
            stay near zero while you are speaking.

    config MIC_LEVEL_LOG
        bool "Log microphone peak level every 3 s"
        default y
        help
            Logs per-channel peaks rather than a combined level: the downmix
            averages L and R, so if the board wires only one ES7210 input, a
            combined meter would just look quiet instead of showing which
            channel is live.



    config AEC_ENABLE
        bool "Cancel the echo in the live capture path"
        default y
        help
            Puts esp-sr's standalone AEC between the microphone and everything
            downstream -- Deepgram, the orb's level, the instrumentation. ON by
            default: this is what makes the device interruptible, and turning it
            off returns it to half duplex.

            It also switches the microphone to the 4-channel TDM capture in
            audio_codecs.c, because the canceller needs the hardware
            echo-reference lane (ES7210 MIC3) that the BSP's own bring-up leaves
            unpowered. With this off, the audio path is byte-for-byte the old
            half-duplex one, which is what makes the A/B measurement in
            docs/notes/echo-cancellation.md repeatable.

            AEC_MODE_FD_LOW_COST, chosen by measurement rather than by the
            published table: 17.3 dB of ERLE on Espressif's own test vectors
            against their own output's 18.3. The SR modes have no non-linear
            stage and scored negative.

            COSTS ~14.7 kB OF INTERNAL RAM, measured on this board rather than
            quoted: free internal 70,260 -> 55,564 B and largest free block
            34,816 -> 22,528 B, steady state with a live session and the gate
            still closed. It also costs about 6.5 fps on the display. The "16
            bytes" this help used to cite was wrong.

            THIS ALONE DOES NOT GIVE BARGE-IN. It cleans the microphone;
            MIC_GATE_WHILE_AGENT_SPEAKS is what decides whether the microphone
            is open. Both are set for full duplex by default. When bringing the
            canceller up on new hardware, turn the gate back ON first -- with the
            gate closed a broken canceller costs nothing.

    config MIC_GATE_WHILE_AGENT_SPEAKS
        bool "Drop captured audio while the agent is speaking"
        default n
        depends on AEC_ENABLE
        help
            The half-duplex gate. OFF by default, which is what lets the agent be
            talked over: the microphone stays open while it speaks and the
            canceller keeps it from hearing itself.

            Turn it ON to go back to half duplex. That is the right move when
            bringing the canceller up on unfamiliar hardware -- with the gate
            closed a canceller that does not work costs nothing, and the ERLE
            can be read from the "mic peak ... out=" log before anything depends
            on it. It is also the correct setting for any board where
            cancellation cannot reach the required ERLE, because an uncancelled
            open microphone during playback is the self-conversation, not
            barge-in.

            Measured on this board, full duplex holds at every volume: 22.4 dB
            mean ERLE at volume 70 and 23.2 dB at 100, barge-in firing at both.

            The prompt follows this symbol -- full-duplex.md when it is off,
            half-duplex.md when it is on -- so the model is never told it can be
            talked over by a build that gates the microphone.


    config AEC_FULL_DUPLEX_MAX_VOLUME
        int "Fall back to half duplex above this speaker volume"
        default 100
        range 20 100
        depends on AEC_ENABLE && !MIC_GATE_WHILE_AGENT_SPEAKS
        help
            An escape hatch that is OFF by default, because the board this was
            written for did not need it.

            The theory it was built for: signal-to-echo ratio at the microphone
            falls with speaker volume, and an archived table predicted this
            board's amplifier going non-linear between 85 and 100 -- ERLE
            collapsing to 19.3 dB against 30.1 required, with the microphone
            nowhere near clipping. On that table, opening the mic at 100 would
            hand back the self-conversation silently.

            MEASURED ON HARDWARE 2026-08-30, THE TABLE DOES NOT HOLD HERE:

              volume 70   22.4 dB mean ERLE over 24 samples, barge-in fires
              volume 100  23.2 dB mean ERLE over 34 samples, barge-in fires

            The two are indistinguishable, and a ~22 minute session at volume
            100 ran with zero allocation failures and zero dropped frames.
            Audible clipping does appear at 100; it does not stop the canceller
            or the interruption.

            So the default is 100, which means the fallback never fires and full
            duplex holds at every volume. The knob is kept rather than deleted
            because the collapse is a real amplifier behaviour that another unit
            or another board may well show -- if barge-in works quietly and
            fails loud, lower this.

            THE FLOOR IS NOISIER THAN THE MEAN. At 100 several blocks scored
            2-10 dB where 70 stayed tighter. Some of those are double-talk,
            where low ERLE is correct. Whether any are genuine failures is not
            settled, and the mean will not tell you -- a missed interruption is
            a floor event.

            Below the threshold nothing here runs. Set it lower and the
            half-duplex gate returns above that volume, at which point the
            device cannot be interrupted while the build-time prompt still tells
            the user it can. That mismatch is why this is not on by default.

    config AEC_UPLINK_VAD
        bool "Only stream the microphone upstream while the agent speaks if someone is talking"
        default y
        depends on AEC_ENABLE && !MIC_GATE_WHILE_AGENT_SPEAKS
        help
            THE FIX FOR THE BANDWIDTH WALL, not for echo.

            With the gate open, the device uplinks 16 kHz mono continuously --
            about 32 kB/s -- straight through the agent's reply, which is the
            moment the TLS write path is already busiest. Measured on this board
            at volume 70: barge-in works, and the link dies anyway. The heap
            probe caught the exact original signature,

              ALLOC FAILED: 1630 bytes caps=0x0000080c (INTERNAL|DMA|8BIT)
              esp-aes: Failed to allocate memory

            eight times, with largest-free-block collapsing 14,336 -> 2,304 B
            inside 600 ms and the session dropping.

            That is a BANDWIDTH problem, not an ERLE problem -- the canceller was
            delivering 22.4 dB mean against 15.2 required. More cancellation
            cannot help. Sending less can.

            So while the agent is speaking, only forward blocks whose POST-AEC
            level clears AEC_UPLINK_VAD_PEAK. With the room quiet the residual
            echo is a few tens of counts and nothing is sent; when someone talks
            over the agent the cancelled signal jumps by an order of magnitude
            and the uplink opens. Normal listening, with the speaker idle, is
            completely unaffected -- this only ever applies during playback.

            The display tap is NOT gated by this: the orb keeps drawing the room.

    config AEC_UPLINK_VAD_PEAK
        int "Post-AEC peak that counts as speech during playback"
        default 400
        range 50 8000
        depends on AEC_UPLINK_VAD
        help
            Peak absolute sample, measured AFTER cancellation, above which a
            block is treated as somebody talking rather than as residual echo.

            Measured on this board at volume 70, from the "mic peak ... out="
            log: with nobody talking over the agent, post-AEC peaks ran 5-40 and
            touched 263 at worst; talking over it produced 533, 1349 and 1607.
            400 sits in that gap, nearer the noise than the speech so a quiet
            barge-in still opens the link.

            Too low and the reply burst keeps the uplink open and the link dies
            as before; too high and quiet interruptions are ignored. Retune from
            the same log line, not by guessing.

    config AEC_UPLINK_VAD_HANGOVER_MS
        int "Keep the uplink open this long after the last speech block"
        default 1000
        range 0 5000
        depends on AEC_UPLINK_VAD
        help
            Detection is retrospective -- a block is judged after it is captured
            -- so the opening consonant of an interruption can be lost, and a
            gate that shut the instant someone paused would chop the rest into
            fragments Deepgram cannot transcribe.

            The hangover holds the uplink open across the pauses inside a
            sentence. It costs bandwidth only for the tail, and only during
            playback. One second is about two of these capture blocks past a
            normal inter-word gap.

    config AEC_REF_GAIN_DB
        int "Echo-reference lane gain (dB)"
        default 12
        range 0 37
        depends on AEC_ENABLE
        help
            Gain on ES7210 MIC3, the echo-reference lane. SEPARATE FROM
            MIC_IN_GAIN, which is device-wide and would otherwise apply here too.

            This is a level-scaling choice with two failure modes, and both were
            measured on this board at CONFIG_AUDIO_OUT_VOLUME=100:

              0 dB  -> reference peaks about 181 against microphones at 3260.
                       That is -45 dBFS, roughly seven and a half bits of the
                       ADC, and it throws away signal-to-noise a canceller needs.
             24 dB  -> reference peaks 13541 against microphones at 11358, i.e.
                       -7.7 dBFS. Well scaled, but only 7.7 dB of headroom before
                       clipping -- and a clipped reference is a nonlinearity in
                       the one signal that has to stay linear.

            12 dB splits them: about -19 dBFS with ~19 dB of headroom. It is a
            reasonable default and NOT a measured optimum.

            Applied AFTER esp_codec_dev_open(), because open() replays the
            device-wide gain over every channel and silently undoes anything set
            before it.

    choice AEC_NLP_LEVEL_CHOICE
        prompt "Non-linear processing aggressiveness"
        depends on AEC_ENABLE
        default AEC_NLP_NORMAL
        help
            How hard the non-linear stage suppresses residual echo. It suppresses
            hardest during DOUBLE-TALK, which is the barge-in moment, so the
            aggressive settings trade away the very thing being built. Worth
            about 1.2 dB, which is a tuning knob and not the fix. Tunable only
            with a person talking over the device.

        config AEC_NLP_NORMAL
            bool "Normal -- least speech damage, most residual echo"
        config AEC_NLP_AGGR
            bool "Aggressive -- esp-sr's own default"
        config AEC_NLP_VERYAGGR
            bool "Very aggressive -- most suppression, most speech damage"
    endchoice

    config AEC_NLP_LEVEL
        int
        depends on AEC_ENABLE
        default 0 if AEC_NLP_NORMAL
        default 1 if AEC_NLP_AGGR
        default 2 if AEC_NLP_VERYAGGR

    config HEAP_PROBE
        bool "Log internal-RAM floors and allocation failures"
        default n
        help
            Names the consumer of internal RAM rather than guessing at it. This
            is the instrument that identified the 1,630 B MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL|
            DMA|8BIT failure behind the abandoned full-duplex attempt: an
            allocation-failure hook that prints the size and caps of the request
            that failed, plus a 50 ms sampler, against the TLM line's 1 s which
            is too coarse to see the collapse.

            DEFAULT OFF. The hook runs in whatever context failed -- possibly an
            ISR -- so it is a debug tool, not a resident.

    config SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Stop the session after this many idle seconds (0 = never)"
        default 15
        range 0 3600
        help
            The device uplinks 16 kHz mono for as long as a session is open --
            roughly 32 kB/s -- and reconnects on its own if the socket drops. A
            board left powered on a desk therefore streams, and bills,
            indefinitely. This stops the session once nothing has happened for
            this long; tapping the screen or the BOOT button starts it again.

            The trade is reconnect latency on the next word. Measured on this
            board, coming back up takes 1.1-6.0 s of CONNECTING plus 0.5 s of
            BUFFERING, so a short timeout costs a noticeable wait before the
            device can hear you.

            Activity means an end-of-turn, Deepgram reporting the user started
            speaking, or the speaker being busy -- not the local microphone level,
            which would tie session lifetime to whether the display came up.

            0 disables the timeout and restores the old always-on behaviour.

    config AUDIO_CAPTURE_ALWAYS
        bool "Keep visualizing the microphone while the session is stopped"
        default n
        help
            BENCH TOOL. Leave this off for anything you intend to use.

            Normally the capture tap is gated with the session, ahead of both the
            display and the upstream sink, because a display still dancing to
            background noise reads as broken rather than as stopped.

            Turning it on removes that gate, which is the only practical way to
            calibrate the microphone: with a live session, speaking makes the
            agent answer, and the half-duplex mic gate then deafens the mic
            for most of the measurement. In one acoustic session ten of twelve
            recorded segments were the agent's own voice and only two were the
            microphone. With this on you can stop the session -- so nothing can
            talk back -- and still read microphone levels off the telemetry.

    config UI_TELEMETRY_MS
        int "Telemetry line interval (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 200 60000
        help
            How often main's loop emits the consolidated TLM line: one
            machine-parseable key=value record of face, behaviour, audio levels,
            frame timing and heap.

            It is emitted from the main task rather than the UI on purpose --
            console writes block, and a 200 byte line at 115200 baud is most of a
            frame, so logging it from the frame timer would corrupt the timings it
            reports.

            1000 ms gives several samples inside a spoken word. Raise it to reduce
            log volume for long unattended runs; note that a window shorter than a
            frame period will report frames=0.

    config UI_SHOW_INDICATORS
        bool "Draw the touch target and what a tap will do"
        default n
        help
            BENCH AND DEMO AID. Leave it off for anything you intend to use.

            The control is invisible. It is a 70 px circle in the middle of a
            466 px panel, with no outline and no label, and the rest of the
            screen does nothing -- which is right for a device sitting on a desk
            and unhelpful for anyone learning it, reviewing it, or filming it.

            With this on, the frame timer draws a thin ring at exactly the hit
            radius, and a line under the status word saying what a tap does RIGHT
            NOW: "interrupt" while the agent is speaking, "stop" or "start"
            otherwise, "advance" during the display test. The ring changes colour
            with it, so the affordance and the action agree.

            IT SHOWS, IT DOES NOT CHANGE. Every gesture behaves identically with
            this off; nothing here is load bearing.

            Costs a frame. The ring is drawn over whatever the face painted and
            its bounding box is invalidated every frame, which the orb otherwise
            avoids -- it invalidates only the dots it moved. The spectrum already
            repaints the whole canvas and pays nothing extra.

    choice UI_DEFAULT_FACE_CHOICE
        prompt "Default display face"
        default UI_DEFAULT_FACE_ORB
        help
            Which visual the device boots with. Either can be switched to at
            runtime by asking the agent out loud, so this only picks the default.

        config UI_DEFAULT_FACE_ORB
            bool "Orb -- dotted shell that reacts to the conversation"
        config UI_DEFAULT_FACE_SPECTRUM
            bool "Spectrum -- radial FFT analyser"
    endchoice

    config UI_DEFAULT_FACE
        int
        default 0 if UI_DEFAULT_FACE_ORB
        default 1 if UI_DEFAULT_FACE_SPECTRUM
        help
            Index into the face table in ui.c. Set by the choice above.

    choice UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_CHOICE
        prompt "Default orb colour"
        default UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_WHITE
        help
            What colour the orb's dots boot as. Any of these can be asked for at
            runtime ("change the colour to teal"), so this only picks the default.

            Named colours rather than a raw hex on purpose: every colour the
            device can boot in is then also one the agent can be asked to return
            to. A hex here could ship a board whose default has no name, and
            "put it back to normal" would have nothing to reach for.

            Affects the orb only. The spectrum colours its bands by frequency and
            by which half of the conversation is live, so it ignores this.

        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_WHITE
            bool "White -- the monochrome shell, and the rasteriser's identity"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_VIRA
            bool "Vira -- soft peach-coral"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_TOMATO
            bool "Tomato -- warm red"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_ORANGE
            bool "Orange"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_YELLOW
            bool "Yellow"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_LIME
            bool "Lime -- sharp yellow-green"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_GREEN
            bool "Green"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_TEAL
            bool "Teal -- vivid aqua"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_CYAN
            bool "Cyan"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_BLUE
            bool "Blue"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_INDIGO
            bool "Indigo -- blue-violet"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_PURPLE
            bool "Purple"
        config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_PINK
            bool "Pink"
    endchoice

    config UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR
        int
        default 0 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_WHITE
        default 1 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_VIRA
        default 2 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_TOMATO
        default 3 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_ORANGE
        default 4 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_YELLOW
        default 5 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_LIME
        default 6 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_GREEN
        default 7 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_TEAL
        default 8 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_CYAN
        default 9 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_BLUE
        default 10 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_INDIGO
        default 11 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_PURPLE
        default 12 if UI_DEFAULT_ORB_COLOR_PINK
        help
            Index into the colour table in orb_colors.c. Set by the choice above,
            and ORDER IS THE CONTRACT -- these have to match that table.

    config ORB_AMP_GAIN_MIC
        int "Display level gain, microphone path (percent)"
        default 600
        range 50 3000
        help
            Scales microphone RMS into the 0-1 level the display animates with.
            Percent, so 600 means 6.0x.

            SEPARATE from the agent gain below because the two signals are not
            comparable: measured on this board, conversational speech peaks
            around 0.095 RMS at the mic while agent playback at full volume
            reaches 0.31 -- a factor of three. One shared gain either pins the
            agent at its ceiling or leaves the microphone barely moving.

            Raise this if speaking normally does not fill the display; lower it
            if room noise animates it while nobody is talking.

    config ORB_AMP_GAIN_AGENT
        int "Display level gain, agent playback path (percent)"
        default 200
        range 50 3000
        help
            As above, for audio on its way to the speaker. Lower than the
            microphone gain because playback is already amplified.

            At the reference's provisional 3.2x this path saturated at 0.994,
            which pins the speaking gesture at its maximum for entire sentences
            and throws away every bit of expression it has.

    config BATTERY
        bool "Read the battery from the AXP2101 power-management IC"
        default y
        help
            The board charges and runs from a single Li-ion cell through an
            AXP2101, which carries its own fuel gauge. With this on, a low
            priority task reads charge, cell voltage and charge direction off
            that chip and hands them to three readers: the dots on the screen,
            the TLM line, and the agent's get_battery function.

            The module only ever READS the AXP2101. That chip also owns the
            rails feeding the AMOLED panel and the codec, so a driver that
            configures regulators is a way to brown the display out; this one
            has no write path at all.

            Off costs nothing: no task, no I2C device, no code.

    config BATTERY_SHOW_DOTS
        bool "Show the charge dots on screen"
        depends on BATTERY
        default y
        help
            Four dots following the display's outer curve from 1 to 2 o'clock,
            each worth a quarter of the charge, filling clockwise. Dots still
            held go bright, spent ones go dim, so 50% reads as two bright then
            two dim. A bolt continues the arc past them while current is going
            into the cell. All of it takes the orb's colour, so set_color moves
            the row too.

            They are drawn while the session is stopped or the display is
            asleep, and whenever the charge is low or the cell is charging. Not
            during a conversation: the face is the point then, and a permanent
            gauge over it is clutter.

            This is separate from UI_SHOW_INDICATORS on purpose. That one is a
            bench aid that draws the invisible touch target; this is a thing an
            owner of the device actually wants.

    config BATTERY_DUMP_REGS
        bool "Log the raw AXP2101 registers on every sample"
        depends on BATTERY
        default n
        help
            BRING-UP TOOL. It prints REG00, REG01, REG34/35 and REGA4 verbatim
            once per sample.

            Turn it on for the first flash on a new board, watch the numbers
            with USB plugged in and again on battery, and confirm the bits move
            the way the register comments claim. Turn it off afterwards -- at
            the default sample interval it is a line every five seconds
            forever, and it says nothing the TLM line does not.

    config BATTERY_SAMPLE_MS
        int "Battery sample interval (ms)"
        depends on BATTERY
        default 5000
        range 500 600000
        help
            Charge is a slow signal and every sample is a transaction on the bus
            that touch, the codec and the RTC also share, so this is deliberately
            far slower than the 1 s TLM line. The TLM line simply repeats the
            last sample between reads.

            Note the filter is a median of five, so the reported percentage
            settles about five intervals after a change.

    config BATTERY_LOW_PCT
        int "Low battery threshold (percent)"
        depends on BATTERY
        default 20
        range 1 90
        help
            At or below this, the dots stay on screen whatever else is
            happening and the device says so once. It clears five points higher,
            so a cell resting on the line does not chatter the warning.

    config BATTERY_CRITICAL_PCT
        int "Critical battery threshold (percent)"
        depends on BATTERY
        default 8
        range 0 50
        help
            At or below this the display is put to sleep and left there: the
            panel is the largest single draw on the board, and dimming it is the
            only lever this firmware has to make the remaining charge last.

            The session is left alone -- a device that hangs up mid-sentence to
            save power is worse than one that goes dark and keeps talking.

            0 disables it.

    config WIFI_SIGNAL
        bool "Report Wi-Fi signal strength"
        default y
        help
            The link is what carries the whole session, and when it degrades the
            only visible symptom is dropped audio -- with nothing in the log
            saying the radio was the cause. With this on, the RSSI of the
            associated AP is read from the driver's own beacon cache and handed
            to three readers: the bars on the screen, the TLM line, and the
            agent's get_signal_strength function.

            There is no sampling task and no scan. esp_wifi_sta_get_ap_info()
            returns a value the driver already has, in microseconds -- a scan,
            by contrast, would starve the link for hundreds of ms, which is why
            the one in wifi_prov.c runs only while there is no session.

            Off costs nothing: no code, no event handler.

    config WIFI_SIGNAL_SHOW_BARS
        bool "Show the signal icon on screen"
        depends on WIFI_SIGNAL
        default y
        help
            The familiar wifi glyph -- a dot with three arcs fanning out above
            it -- at 10 to 11 o'clock, balancing the battery dots across the
            panel. Four elements for four bars: the dot alone is one, each arc
            past it is one more, which is the progression every phone draws.
            Lit parts go bright, the rest go dim. It takes the orb's colour, so
            set_color moves it too.

            Shown while the session is stopped, the display is asleep, or the
            signal is weak. Not during a conversation: the face is the point
            then, and a permanent gauge over it is clutter.

    config WIFI_SIGNAL_WEAK_DBM
        int "Weak-signal warning threshold (dBm)"
        depends on WIFI_SIGNAL
        default -80
        range -99 -30
        help
            The driver raises WIFI_EVENT_STA_BSS_RSSI_LOW when the beacon RSSI
            falls past this, and the handler logs one line per excursion. The
            point is to put a cause in the log immediately BEFORE the dropped
            audio that follows, at a moment the TLM cadence might otherwise
            miss.

            One line per excursion, not per crossing: the threshold is one-shot,
            so re-arming it while the link is still below fires it again about
            once a second. See s_weak_logged in wifi_sta.c.

            -80 dBm is roughly where a 2.4 GHz link starts retrying enough to
            matter to a 16 kHz stream. Raise it toward -70 to be warned earlier.

endmenu
